+## Subtopics
+
+### Spatial Partitioning
+SUDS splits the scene into $K$ spatial cells to enable distributed training and memory management. It differs from global NeRFs by allowing independent optimization of local regions, which is critical for scaling to city-sized areas [S1].
+
+### Shadow Modeling
+SUDS explicitly models a shadow ratio $\rho_d$ in the dynamic branch to scale down static color. This distinguishes it from methods that ignore shadows or model them implicitly, improving depth reconstruction and static/dynamic factorization [S1].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Scale:** SUDS scales to tens of thousands of objects across 1.2 million frames from 1700 videos spanning geospatial footprints of hundreds of kilometers [S1].
-*   **Training Speed:** SUDS is 10x quicker to train than state-of-the-art methods that rely on ground truth 3D bounding box annotations [S1].
-*   **Memory:** Unlike object-centric approaches like NSG which require over 1TB of memory for a 30-second video, SUDS uses hash tables to manage memory efficiently [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Handles ego-motion and multi-video representations; uses unlabeled inputs (RGB, LiDAR, flow) for supervision; enables unsupervised 3D instance segmentation and cuboid detection [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires careful tuning of loss weights; relies on self-supervised 2D features (DINO) which may have noise; spatial partitioning requires visibility reasoning to prune irrelevant rays [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Training is 10x quicker than state-of-the-art methods relying on ground truth 3D bounding box annotations [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Scales to tens of thousands of objects across 1.2 million frames from 1700 videos spanning hundreds of kilometers [S1]. Memory usage is controlled by multi-resolution hash tables and spatial partitioning, avoiding the linear memory growth of per-object NeRFs (e.g., NSG requires >1TB for 30s video) [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Handles ego-motion and multi-video representations; uses unlabeled inputs (RGB, LiDAR, flow); supports semantic decomposition and 3D scene flow [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Requires aligned sparse LiDAR and optical flow for supervision; performance depends on the quality of self-supervised 2D descriptors (DINO) [S1].
 
 ## Background
-SUDS (Scalable Urban Dynamic Scenes) was introduced in 2023 as a method for learning a global representation of urban scenes that facilitates free-viewpoint rendering, semantic decomposition, and 3D scene flow at arbitrary poses and time steps using multiple video inputs [S1]. It addresses the limitations of prior dynamic scene representations that require supervised inputs like panoptic segmentation labels or bounding boxes, which are difficult to acquire for in-the-wild captures [S1]. The method builds on Instant-NGP's multi-resolution hash tables and extends them to support 4D spacetime indexing and multi-video representations [S1]. It is used for large-scale dynamic NeRF reconstruction, enabling tasks such as novel-view synthesis, unsupervised 3D instance segmentation, and unsupervised 3D cuboid detection [S1].
+SUDS (Scalable Urban Dynamic Scenes) was introduced in 2023 [S1]. It addresses the limitations of prior dynamic scene representations that required supervised inputs like panoptic segmentation labels or bounding boxes, which are difficult to acquire for in-the-wild captures [S1]. It builds on Instant-NGP's multi-resolution hash tables and extends them to 4D spacetime with explicit shadow modeling and semantic distillation [S1].
 
